WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.230
Now, onto the secondary pollutants.

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.570
Ozone is created in the atmosphere.

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:09.090
The full chemical story is extraordinarily complex.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:13.770
But a simple version involves three precursors-- nitrogen oxide,

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:17.710
carbon monoxide, and volatile organic compounds.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:21.800
When combined with sunlight, this mixture creates ozone.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.770
These volatile organic compounds can be anything from gasoline

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:27.710
that evaporates from the gas tanks of cars

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:31.490
to isoprenes-- organic compounds released by trees.

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:33.370
Ozone chemistry is surprising.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.700
In general, when one mixes oxidizers and hydrocarbons,

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.850
one expects to reduce the amount of oxidizer.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:44.260
When the origin of ground level ozone-- also known as smog-- around Los Angeles

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.440
was first discovered in the 1950s, it was a big surprise

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.500
to chemists-- a mixture of oxidizers and hydrocarbons

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.510
is being cooked in the atmosphere to produce

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.980
ozone and even more powerful oxidizer.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.270
It&amp;#39;s worth mentioning that the ozone layer in the stratosphere

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:00.720
is something we want.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.000
It protects the surface of the Earth from UV

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.129
that would otherwise sterilize the planet.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:07.810
On the other hand, ozone in the lower atmosphere

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:10.820
is something we do not want because ozone breaks down

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.580
essentially any organic compound it touches--

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.880
from the service of your lungs to the plastic parts on your car

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.980
or the growing parts of plants.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:20.900
Stratospheric ozone, good.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.800
Surface ozone, bad.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.450
Controlling ozone is complicated because the amount of ozone produced

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:30.450
depends in a sensitive way on the mixture of the precursors.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.630
In some conditions, cutting NOx emissions

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:35.520
can actually lead do an increase in ozone.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.520
Perhaps because of this complexity, the US

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.340
has made only very moderate progress on reducing ozone exposure in cities--

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:45.750
roughly a 20% reduction since 1980.

